        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: High-Quality Rendering Architecture Explained -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          How Our Engine Achieves PDF to Image High Quality
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          Standard web converters often render PDF documents at default 72 DPI screen resolutions. When magnified on modern 4K monitors or mobile Retina displays, low-DPI images look blurry, pixelated, and illegible.
        </p>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          Our <strong>pdf to image high quality</strong> engine solves this by employing a dual-multiplier rendering viewport:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-orange-500 font-extrabold text-lg">2.0×</span>
              Super-Sampled Viewport Matrix
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              Every PDF point is mapped to 2 physical device pixels before canvas drawing. For a standard A4 or US Letter page (595 × 842 points), the output image is rendered at 1190 × 1684 pixels at 192 DPI, ensuring that small footnotes and table indices remain completely readable.
